---

#### 2. Screen reader blind spot: Arrow key navigation does not announce active option
- **Location**: [`BuggyDropdown.jsx:25-33`](file:///BuggyDropdown.jsx#L25-L33), [`BuggyDropdown.jsx:53-72`](file:///BuggyDropdown.jsx#L53-L72)
- **Elements Involved**: `<ul role="listbox">`, `<li role="option">`
- **User Group Impacted**: Screen reader users (VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS)
- **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (Level A), WAI-ARIA APG *Listbox Pattern* (Keyboard Navigation & Active Descendant)
- **Confidence**: HIGH
- **Why this matters**: Focus is placed statically on `<ul tabIndex="0">`. When the user presses `ArrowDown` or `ArrowUp`, `selectedIndex` updates in state, modifying `aria-selected` and CSS classes on the child `<li>` elements. However, focus never moves to individual `<li>` nodes, nor does `<ul role="listbox">` manage `aria-activedescendant`. Screen readers do not broadcast attribute changes on un-focused sibling/child elements. The screen reader user hears silence while arrowing through the list.
- **Fix**:
  Assign unique IDs to each option (e.g. `id={`dropdown-opt-${index}`}`). On the `<ul>`, add `aria-activedescendant={isOpen ? `dropdown-opt-${selectedIndex}` : undefined}`.

